    hero: {
      title: 'HYAKUNIN ISSHU',
      subtitle: 'Card Game',
    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Hyakunin Isshu',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Hyakunin Isshu (ç™¾äººä¸€é¦– - 'One Hundred People, One Poem Each') is a traditional Japanese card game based on a classical anthology of waka poems. It is played as a competitive game called 'Karuta'. Players must match a poem being read aloud with the correct 'grabbing' card spread out on the floor. It is a game of extreme speed, memory, and auditory processing.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "The anthology was compiled by Fujiwara no Teika in the 13th century. The game form, 'Kyogu Karuta', developed during the Edo period.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Typically 1 vs 1 in competitive play.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '100 poetry cards. In a match, only 50 are used (25 for each player).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Uta-garuta deck (200 cards: 100 Yomifuda reading cards, 100 Torifuda grabbing cards), playing mat or flat surface, reader (yomite) to read the poems.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Shuffle the 100 Torifuda (grabbing cards). Each player places 25 cards in their territory (face-up). The remaining 50 Torifuda are not used in that round. The Yomifuda (reading deck) is kept separate for the reader.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Kyōgi Karuta (Competitive): Official competitive rules: 50 Torifuda in play (25 per player), reader reads from full Yomifuda set, strict otetsuki penalty, national rules (e.g. All Japan Karuta Association).',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Casual Uta-garuta: Recreational play: may use fewer cards, relaxed reading speed, or team play with multiple players.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Genpei War (Team): Team variant: two teams face each other; reader reads; first correct touch from either team wins the card for that team.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
